----------------------------------------------
Running mcpltool -h
----------------------------------------------
Tool for inspecting or modifying Monte Carlo Particle List (.mcpl) files.

Usage:
  python -m mcpl [options] FILE                  Show the header and first particles
  python -m mcpl -S [options] FILE               Summary of the particles
  python -m mcpl -w HIST [options] FILE [...]    Histogram in a window
  python -m mcpl -t HIST [options] FILE [...]    Histogram in the terminal
  python -m mcpl -x [options] FILE OUT.mcpl.gz   Extract particles into a new file
  python -m mcpl -m OUT.mcpl.gz FILE1 FILE2 ...  Merge compatible files
  python -m mcpl -r FILE                         Repair a file not properly closed

Common options:
  -lN, -sN       Use only N particles, or skip the first N particles.
  -e EDIT        Pick and change particles, e.g.
                 -e "pdgcode == neutron; z += 1cm; ekin < 25meV".
  -o FILE        Save the histogram in FILE (.png, .pdf, or .svg with -w, .txt
                 with -t).
  -d             Dry run: do everything except writing files.

Examples:
  python -m mcpl -w "ekin/MeV;logx;display_edit=pdgcode==neutron" file.mcpl.gz
  python -m mcpl -x -e "pdgcode == neutron" file.mcpl.gz neutrons.mcpl.gz
  python -m mcpl -S file.mcpl.gz

More help:
  --full-help              All options.
  -e help, --hist-help     How to write EDIT and HIST.

